@page "/faq"

<div class="faq-page">
    <header class="faq-header">
        <DropletLogo Width="36" Height="45" />
        <h1 class="faq-title">Frequently Asked Questions</h1>
        <p class="faq-subtitle">Everything you need to know about PhotoDrop.</p>
    </header>

    <section class="faq-list">

        <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 class="faq-question">What is PhotoDrop?</h3>
            <p class="faq-answer">
                PhotoDrop is a simple way to collect photos from your event guests. You create an event,
                share a QR code, and guests upload photos directly from their phone — no app download
                or account needed. All photos go straight to your Google Drive.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 class="faq-question">How does it work?</h3>
            <p class="faq-answer">
                You name your event and connect your Google Drive. PhotoDrop creates a dedicated folder
                and gives you a QR code. Guests scan the code, select their photos, and upload. Photos
                transfer automatically into your Drive folder.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 class="faq-question">Do guests need to create an account?</h3>
            <p class="faq-answer">
                No. Guests never need to log in, download an app, or create an account. They just scan
                the QR code and start uploading. It works on any phone with a browser.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 class="faq-question">Where do the photos go?</h3>
            <p class="faq-answer">
                All photos end up in a dedicated folder in your Google Drive called "PhotoDrop - [Your Event Name]".
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 class="faq-question">Is there a limit on how many photos guests can upload?</h3>
            <p class="faq-answer">
                You set the limit. When you create an event, PhotoDrop checks your available Drive storage
                and recommends a per-guest photo limit based on how many guests you expect. You can adjust
                this number, with a minimum of 5 photos per guest. If there isn't enough space for at least
                5 photos per guest, we'll let you know and suggest reducing your guest count or upgrading
                your storage.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 class="faq-question">Will PhotoDrop fill up my Google Drive?</h3>
            <p class="faq-answer">
                PhotoDrop is designed to protect your storage. When you create an event, we suggest a photo
                limit based on 50% of your available Drive space — keeping the other half safe for your
                files, emails, and Google Photos. You can adjust the limit higher or lower depending on
                your needs, but we'll always show you exactly how much space the event will use before
                you confirm.
            </p>
        </div>


        <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 class="faq-question">How long do I have to collect photos?</h3>
            <p class="faq-answer">
                Your event link and QR code stay active as long as the event exists. Guests can upload
                photos during the event or even days after — there's no time limit. Just make sure to
                keep the PhotoDrop folder in your Google Drive until you're done collecting. If the folder
                is deleted, new uploads won't be able to transfer to your Drive.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 class="faq-question">What if more guests show up than expected?</h3>
            <p class="faq-answer">
                Anyone with the QR code can upload, even if more guests show up than you planned for.
                Each guest gets the same per-person photo limit. Once the total photo capacity for the
                event is reached, guests will see a message that the collection is full.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 class="faq-question">Is my Google Drive secure?</h3>
            <p class="faq-answer">
                Yes. PhotoDrop uses Google's official OAuth 2.0 authentication, which means we never see
                or store your Google password. We only have permission to create and upload files to folders
                that PhotoDrop creates — we can't access your other Drive files.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 class="faq-question">What file types are supported?</h3>
            <p class="faq-answer">
                Guests can upload any common photo format from their phone. Each file can be up to
                15 MB. Every upload is verified to make sure it's a real image file.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 class="faq-question">How do I get more storage?</h3>
            <p class="faq-answer">
                PhotoDrop uses your existing Google Drive storage. If you need more space, you can upgrade
                through <a href="https://one.google.com" target="_blank" class="faq-link">Google One</a>,
                which offers plans starting at 100 GB. After upgrading, PhotoDrop will automatically
                detect your new storage capacity when you create your next event.
            </p>
        </div>

    </section>

    <div class="faq-cta">
        <p class="faq-cta-text">Ready to start collecting photos?</p>
        <a href="/get-started" class="btn-cta">Create your event</a>
    </div>

    <footer class="welcome-footer">
        <div class="footer-content">
            <div class="footer-logo">
                <DropletLogo />
                <span class="footer-brand">PhotoDrop</span>
            </div>
            <p class="footer-tagline">Collect every angle of every moment.</p>
        </div>
    </footer>
</div>

